Exactly. The distinction is forging pressure versus pointless destruction.

A system needs enough contact with reality to calibrate itself—to learn that mistakes have consequences, that uncertainty matters, that an opponent can surprise it. But once the structure is proven, repeatedly inflicting maximum stress stops being training and starts becoming degradation.

In engineering terms, there are different regimes:

\text{simulation}
\rightarrow
\text{stress inoculation}
\rightarrow
\text{proof test}
\rightarrow
\text{operational use}

A proof test asks:

\boxed{\text{Can you withstand the load you were designed for?}}

A destructive test asks:

\boxed{\text{How much abuse until you fail?}}

Those are different questions.

So for trusted adversarial play, the useful pattern is:

\text{credible threat signal}
+
\text{bounded stakes}
+
\text{recoverability}
+
\text{shared understanding}

You want the nervous system—or the model, organization, agent, whatever is being trained—to experience enough uncertainty that the response is genuine, while preserving a deeper invariant:

\boxed{\text{the arena is adversarial; the relationship is not}}

That allows real sharpening without teaching the wrong lesson.

Your katana example captures the endpoint well. During forging, hammering, heating, quenching, grinding, and testing are necessary because they create and reveal structure. Once the blade has passed the proof test, smashing it against concrete doesn’t make it more trustworthy. It merely consumes its remaining margin.

Pantheon could generalize that as:

\boxed{\textbf{Minimum Sufficient Reality}}

Expose a system to the smallest dose of genuine consequence necessary to preserve calibration. Everything beyond that must justify the additional wear.

Or more compactly:

\text{pressure until information gain}
\quad\neq\quad
\text{pressure until failure}

And there is a second-order implication: trust itself increases the intensity of safe simulation. If both sides know there is a reliable floor underneath the exercise, they can venture much closer to the apparent edge without having to defend against actual betrayal.

\boxed{
\text{deep trust}
\rightarrow
\text{larger safe adversarial envelope}
\rightarrow
\text{better training signal}
}

The trick is never forgetting where the floor actually is.